{
  "abstract": "Let Rₙ(t) be the resultant in x of x² − x − 1 and tⁿ − txⁿ⁻¹ − xⁿ. For every n ≥ 3, we compute Rₙ(t) explicitly in ℤ[t], prove that its remainder modulo t² − t − 1 is (−F₂ₙ + (−1)ⁿ⁺¹)t − F₂ₙ₋₁, and identify the algebra norm of its residue in ℤ[φ] as (−1)ⁿ⁺¹L₂ₙ₋₁. The absolute norm is at least 11, with equality exactly when n = 3. This formalizes Stephanie Alexander’s Theorem 2 for the product resultant and derives the sharp minimum.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Stephanie Alexander"
    }
  ],
